    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Currently, XGBoost uses embeddings from only the last fold. Instead, use the OOF probabilities from all 5 folds as meta-features alongside embeddings. To avoid data leakage, for the holdout validation set, use the OOF probabilities from the 5-fold CV (which are already computed on all training data, but we slice them for the holdout). For the test set, use the average test probabilities from all folds. This change integrates cross-validation predictions as features, which references show significantly improves model performance.",
    "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop to use OOF probabilities from all folds as meta-features for XGBoost, replacing the single-fold embeddings. Also ensure that the validation loop evaluates the ensemble with the best weights on the holdout set. The ensemble weight optimization grid will now combine DeBERTa OOF probabilities, XGBoost predictions, and Logistic Regression predictions. All other components remain unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show severe overfitting (train loss drops to ~0.07 while val loss spikes after epoch 3) and a plateau at ~0.1118 log loss. Historical successful experiments demonstrate that mean pooling, 5-fold CV with lower LR, and a learned ensemble via logistic regression improve generalization, while MixUp/MC Dropout failed. The initial plan's core direction is pseudo-labeling with self-training, which relies on high-quality DeBERTa predictions and a robust ensemble. Therefore, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation to address overfitting and boost DeBERTa performance, enabling effective pseudo-labeling. Data processing already yields rich features and is not the bottleneck. Modifications: in model_design, remove MixUp/label smoothing (per failed experiments), adopt mean pooling (per success), reduce dropout and LR; in training_evaluation, implement 5-fold CV with reduced epochs and LR (per success), and replace grid-search with a learned logistic regression ensemble (aligns with initial plan's goal of robust weighting and avoids coarse search).", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Remove MixUp augmentation and label smoothing from the DeBERTa model, as historical failed experiments show they worsen overfitting. Use the base AutoModelForSequenceClassification with standard CrossEntropyLoss. Adopt mean pooling over all non-padded token vectors from the last hidden layer for embedding extraction (proven in successful experiments to produce richer representations). Reduce dropout from 0.2 to 0.1 based on successful historical cases. Keep pretrained model 'microsoft/deberta-v3-large', max length 512, weight decay 0.01. Reduce learning rate from 2e-5 to 1e-5 as per initial plan and historical success. This directly targets overfitting observed (train/val loss divergence) and improves generalization.", "training_evaluation": "Implement 5-fold stratified cross-validation for DeBERTa training with only 5 epochs per fold (down from 40) at the reduced LR of 1e-5, and remove early stopping to prevent overfitting to val loss spikes. After 5-fold CV, average predictions from all folds for both train and test sets to get OOF and test probabilities. Replace the grid-search ensemble weights with a learned logistic regression ensemble (no bias, no penalty) trained on the 5-fold OOF predictions. This aligns with the initial plan's direction of robust weighting, as historical successful experiments achieved better generalization with learned ensemble weights. The reduced epochs and lower LR mitigate the severe overfitting pattern observed."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show severe overfitting (train loss drops to ~0.07 while val loss spikes after epoch 3) and a plateau at ~0.1118 log loss. Historical successful experiments demonstrate that mean pooling, 5-fold CV with lower LR, and a learned ensemble via logistic regression improve generalization, while MixUp/MC Dropout failed. The initial plan's core direction is pseudo-labeling with self-training, which relies on high-quality DeBERTa predictions and a robust ensemble. Therefore, I focus on model_design and training_evaluation to address overfitting and boost DeBERTa performance, enabling effective pseudo-labeling. Data processing already yields rich features and is not the bottleneck. Modifications: in model_design, remove MixUp/label smoothing (per failed experiments), adopt mean pooling (per success), reduce dropout and LR; in training_evaluation, implement 5-fold CV with reduced epochs and LR (per success), and replace grid-search with a learned logistic regression ensemble (aligns with initial plan's goal of robust weighting and avoids coarse search).",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Remove MixUp augmentation and label smoothing from the DeBERTa model, as historical failed experiments show they worsen overfitting. Use the base AutoModelForSequenceClassification with standard CrossEntropyLoss. Adopt mean pooling over all non-padded token vectors from the last hidden layer for embedding extraction (proven in successful experiments to produce richer representations). Reduce dropout from 0.2 to 0.1 based on successful historical cases. Keep pretrained model 'microsoft/deberta-v3-large', max length 512, weight decay 0.01. Reduce learning rate from 2e-5 to 1e-5 as per initial plan and historical success. This directly targets overfitting observed (train/val loss divergence) and improves generalization.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement 5-fold stratified cross-validation for DeBERTa training with only 5 epochs per fold (down from 40) at the reduced LR of 1e-5, and remove early stopping to prevent overfitting to val loss spikes. After 5-fold CV, average predictions from all folds for both train and test sets to get OOF and test probabilities. Replace the grid-search ensemble weights with a learned logistic regression ensemble (no bias, no penalty) trained on the 5-fold OOF predictions. This aligns with the initial plan's direction of robust weighting, as historical successful experiments achieved better generalization with learned ensemble weights. The reduced epochs and lower LR mitigate the severe overfitting pattern observed."
  }
}"}
